we're getting to a point in video games  with some long-running series up to the  eighth ninth or tenth iterations one of  these is Assassin's Creed which has not  one but two games coming out this week  three if you include assassin's creed  chronicles china which is coming within  the unity season pass franchises are an  obvious attraction for major publishers  as a reliable source of revenue big  names like Assassin's Creed and call of  duty have capitalized on this revenue  with regular annual releases but it's  not always easy to keep things new and  fresh sometimes franchises will tweak a  formula in a way that will hugely  disappoint like resident evil 6 but  sometimes they constantly change and  constantly shine through like Mario  Galaxy and Super Mario 3d World  obviously all of this is hugely  subjective but here are some things I  think work really well to keep a  franchise feeling fresh first off  location a change of scenery is a pretty  good and common way to get players to  return to your franchise with the  exception of the SCO trilogy Assassin's  Creed pretty much does this every year  the latest location has you leaving the  open seas of the Caribbean for  traversing revolutionary France games  like far cry and the elder scrolls  change up the regions and provinces you  can explore with each installment but  this also happens in games without open  worlds massive actor Knights of the Old  Republic put emphasis on visiting new  planets and galaxies with each new title  and in the case of competitive games  like worms Street Fighter and smash  brothers the location and theme of the  stages filled with all sorts of weird  and wonderful touches are ways a big  deal Bioshock took itself from under the  sea to up in the clouds uncharted took  itself from jungle to snow to desert and  Diablo went from Hell to  all too okay that example but a drastic  location change is a great way of  keeping a good game interesting without  sacrificing too many familiar core  mechanics some games decide to also not  just the location but also the time  period started shooting dudes or  stabbing them doesn't feel too samey  cake battlefield for example which often  changes up its historical period the  shooter series is featured installments  that take place in World War 2 all the  way to the distant future and everything  in between the stuff that makes the game  recognizably battlefield is still there  but the altered setting allows the  environment weapons vehicles and combat  mechanics to undergo interesting  transformations heading further down the  narrative rabbit hole is a great reason  to stick with a story-driven franchise  but there's also a lot to be said for  evolving mechanics Mass Effect is a  really good example of a game that  adapted a lot over the course of its  trilogy to keep up with its growing fan  base I personally preferred when it was  a bit more complex and more like a  traditional RPG with the dense skill  assignment feature in Mass Effect 1 but  others like the way bioware committed to  giving the action more focus in its  sequels since you know the combat is all  about shooting space guns some people  were even weird enough to fall in love  with the competitive multiplayer in 3  we've seen games like Mario Brothers  spearhead technological changes jumping  into 3d and introducing the analog stick  with super mario 64 implementing analog  triggers in a big way with the flood  water pump in super mario sunshine and  perspective puzzles in Super Mario 3d  World on the 3ds we've also seen stuff  like spec ops changeup it's realistic  military shooter roots to adopt a daring  new narrative focused direction with its  11th installment in 2012 spec ops the  line whether a franchise decides to  differentiate itself from the trends or  go with them it's interesting to see how  a series carves out its narrative and  mechanical footing over the course of  several games in the interests of  keeping things interesting and if all  else fails  your franchise is getting dangerously  stale there's always space oh yes  civilization mario galaxy that one call  of duty leveling ghosts everyone loved  lego batman three Angry Birds as always  space seems to be the answer that was a  joke sort of let me know down in the  comments which franchise do you think is  still killing it after all this time and  which franchise you reckon we should  probably kill off and forget about I  will see you next week
 

